 # Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians
 
-Also called: Gaussian Ray Marching, OTS Marcher, 3DGS Marcher
 
 ## Overview
-Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians is a volumetric rendering method that evaluates the full Gaussian mixture along camera rays to accurately handle primitive overlap and compute the volumetric integral, contrasting with the rasterization-based splatting approach of [Gaussian Splatting](946-gaussian-splatting.md). It solves the problem of approximations in standard 3DGS, such as ignoring spatial overlap and incorrect self-attenuation, by performing adaptive sampling and analytical integration [S1]. Unlike splatting, which projects Gaussians to screen space and blends them, this method traverses the 3D scene directly, ensuring that overlapping primitives are resolved with precision governed by sample count rather than rasterization approximations [S1].
